### Page 16 of 17

```text
From: Neal Levy <zelic51@yahoo.com>
Sent: Wednesday, February 17, 2021 2:37 PM
To: CPalma@SteilSurvey.com <CPalma@SteilSurvey.com>; Connor White <cwhite@cheyennecity.org>;
Seth Lloyd <slloyd@cheyennecity.org>
Subject: Zone Change Application for Mission Village Sr Housing Access

These are my comments and questions about the re-zoning of the
strip of land adjacent to our parcel and Sarah Ryschon's parcel
to be used for access to the Mission Village Senior Housing
property:

1. Is there not presently an easement allowing entry from
Griffith, along the north end of the Northwoods Church property,
for service of the electrical lines and cable lines in this
area? Will the easement be set aside for this zoning and
annexation? How will service personnel now access these
utilities?

2. It took more than 12 months for the construction of the
existing Mission Village apartments - a year's worth of
ceaseless loud noise of construction and construction vehicles
beeping in reverse, swirling dirt spread through the
neighborhood, a torn up and impassable sidewalk along the Dell
Range frontage - and as one walks by even now, it is apparent
that a great deal of frontage landscaping has never been
completed. The curb view of the collection area and the east
side of the property looks like some abandoned industrial
drainage pit, and fiber logs still line the driveway and the
sidewalk adjacent to Gater Industries. Will this project also
take so long, be so loud and prevent walking on Dell Range, and
will there be more care taken to actually finish the landscaping
and the condition of the frontage?

3. First it was the daily day-long crying and screaming of the
Harvest Church daycare kids on their playground right next to
and above our privacy fence (an action taken by Harvest without
public review or discussion). Then Mission Village was built and
we now have 3/4 of a full half-round amphitheater echoing and
amplifying noise directly into our back yard, and directly at
our back deck, which used to provide quiet, private comfort.
Mission Village also includes children who play loudly, and
adults who happen to enjoy partying and booming their loud bass
from their vehicles in the back parking lot of the apartment on
warm nights.

I'm sure those living in the Senior Housing probably won't be
adding to the noise, but the construction will undoubtedly
```

